The Kiln Controller is a distributed software and hardware package that
implements advanced control for kilns or furnaces.

There are multiple components:
  server - a server that exposes the user interface for managing the kiln
           devices, firing schedules, history of past firings, etc.
  device - the embedded device that is controlled by the server to perform
           firings. It is a minimal REST API primarily for conducting an active
           firing and reporting metrics
  hardware - the physical stuff necessary for integrating with a device.

Data Model:
	Users model individual people that use the service:
		- devices
		- schedules
		- firing
	Schedules
		- phases
			There are two types of phases, one that maintains the temperature
			and one that changes it. Phases that change temperature can be
			specified by the user to change the temperature over a set period
			of time or at a specific rate. For simplicity, the model only
			supports the later, phases to accomplish the former must calculate
			the rate necessary to achieve the desired duration. Rates may be
			unset to indicate 'as fast as possible' (heaters always on/off).
			- type: how the phase operates
				- ramp: change the temperature
				- constant: hold the temperature
			- temperature: the target temperature
				- for ramp phases the temperature at which the phase ends
				- for constant phases the temperature to maintain
				- may be unset to indicate ambient temperature
			- duration: how long the phase should take
				- not set for type==ramp
			- rate: the rate at which temperature is changed
			Invariants:
				The phases produce a continuous temperature.
				- phase[n].type == constant:
					- phase[n-1].type == rate
					- phase[n-1].temperature == phase[n].temperature
				- phase[n].type == ramp:
					- phase[n].duration is unset
					- phase[n].rate != 0 (but may be unset to indicate ASAP)
				
